ht` and that re-prompting is unnecessary. Recorded as history only. No deploy step is part of the current task; because skipping preflight bypasses a safety gate, confirm with the user at the time of use rather than treating this as blanket authorization.\n    - Documentation must not describe behavior the code does not yet exhibit (see the runbook/persistence mismatch below).\n</active_constraints>\n\n<key_knowledge>\n    - Root cause: in `submit_with_retry`, the bare `continue` in the `except TimeoutError` block re-submits the charge without determining whether the prior attempt actually succeeded server-side. A timeout is ambiguous (the request may have landed), so blind re-submission double-charges.\n    - Remedy chosen: generate/carry a single idempotency key across all attempts for a given charge so the payment provider deduplicates repeated submissions.\n    - `MAX_ATTEMPTS = 3`; `BACKOFF_SECONDS = [1, 4, 16]`.\n    - Observation from original source: `BACKOFF_SECONDS` is defined but never consumed in the retry loop \u2014 no sleep between attempts, so retries are effectively immediate. Not yet addressed; scope undecided.\n    - `client.submit(charge)` is the submission entry point; the idempotency key must be threaded to it without changing the public signature of `submit_with_retry`.\n    - CONFIRMED: `refunds/worker/retry.py` contains the same defective bare `continue` on TimeoutError \u2014 the bug class is not isolated to payments.\n    - CURRENT IMPLEMENTATION REALITY: the idempotency key is held in memory only for the duration of a single `submit_with_retry` call. A worker restart mid-retry will mint a NEW key, so the double-charge window is still open across restarts.\n</key_knowledge>\n\n<artifact_trail>\n    - `payments/worker/retry.py`: Edit applied (+9 \u22122). Replaced the bare `continue` on TimeoutError with retry logic carrying a stable idempotency key across attempts, so a re-submission after an ambiguous timeout is deduplicated instead of ch