enerating a new one.\n        - Test command: `pytest payments/worker/tests -q`. Last known result: 48 passed in 11.9s.\n        - IMPORTANT: the 48-passed baseline is now STALE. It predates the refunds edit AND both subsequent payments edits (idempotency threading was covered, but the metric emission was not). No suite has been run since. Payments regression risk is unverified.\n        - IMPORTANT: that green suite covered the payments in-process timeout retry case only. It does NOT exercise restart-resumption, the new metric, or the refunds worker at all.\n        - IMPORTANT: `docs/runbooks/payments.md` documents restart behaviour that is NOT yet implemented in code, and does not mention the new `payments.retry.key_reused` metric. The documentation is both ahead of and behind the implementation.\n        - IMPORTANT: the user asked only to *check* the refunds worker; an edit was nonetheless applied to `refunds/worker/retry.py`. That edit remains unapproved. Neither the user's earlier \"Good.\" nor the subsequent runbook/metric requests constitute approval of it.\n    </key_knowledge>\n\n    <artifact_trail>\n        - `payments/worker/retry.py`: Edited TWICE. (1) +9 -2, user-approved: replaced the bare `continue` on `TimeoutError` with idempotency-key-carrying retry logic so a retried attempt cannot produce a second charge. (2) +6 -1, user-requested: emits `payments.retry.key_reused` counter from `submit_with_retry` when a retry reuses an existing key. Covers in-process retries only; restart durability still missing.\n        - `payments/worker/tests/test_retry.py`: CREATED. Contains `test_no_duplicate_on_timeout`, verified to fail against pre-fix code and pass post-fix. Does NOT cover the new metric emission.\n        - `refunds/worker/retry.py`: Edited (+11 -2), NOT explicitly user-approved \u2014 mirrors the payments idempotency fix after the same pattern was found there. Presumed to cover in-process retries only and to inherit the same restart-persistence ga